"""dt_log -> dt_map chain mapper (SAMPLE).

Builds the DT map from the DT transfer log so a bonding plan can be drawn on it.
The map is DERIVED: `dt_log` is the record, `dt_map` is a projection of it. Nothing
should ever write `dt_map` by hand -- if it does, a replay of this rule silently
disagrees with it.

[Why the map key is `dt_job` and not `dt_lot`/`dt_slot`]

`dt_lot` and `dt_slot` are the AMBIGUOUS columns in `dt_log` -- they are the thing
enrichment has to infer (the operator moves wafers between tape slot and DT slot by
hand, and split/merge renames lots underneath). `dt_job` is certain: it is the DT work
unit, recorded as `<equipment>_<time>_<tapeslot>`.

Keying a map on a value we are still inferring would put a guess inside an identity.
Every consumer would then bind to a key that changes when the guess is corrected, and
correcting it would look like the map moved. So the map is keyed on what is known, and
the lot/slot attribution rides on top as ordinary enrichment.

[RESOLVED 2026-08-01 -- how a chain addresses a single map cell]

The earlier draft of this file asked the question and got the answer wrong in code.
What was measured:

  * A map table does NOT hold many rows per `business_key_val`. `dt_map` declares
    `composite_key_source = [dt_job, dt_x, dt_y]`, so one business key IS one cell.
    The "437 rows under one key" reading confused the MAP key (`map_key_columns`,
    which scopes a whole map) with the ROW key.
  * `crud.apply_batch_updates` composes that composite key FOR you
    (crud.py:1051-1059) -- but only when the item omits `business_key_val` AND
    supplies every composite source column in `updates`. Supplying
    `business_key_val = dt_job` instead, as the draft did, addresses ONE row per job
    and collapses every cell of that job onto it.
  * So this mapper deliberately does NOT set `business_key_val`. Letting crud compose
    the key also guarantees the mapper cannot disagree with the recomposition crud
    performs after the write (crud.py:1237-1265).

[The remaining limitation -- why this rule still ships disabled]

A chain can UPSERT map cells. It cannot PURGE them. `replace_map` lives on the batch
(schemas.py:138) and `chain_ingestion_worker.py:437-441` builds that batch itself and
never sets it, so a chain has no way to express "this map is now exactly these cells".
Cells from a superseded version of a job would survive forever alongside the new ones.

That is tolerable only if a `dt_job`'s cell set never shrinks. Confirm that before
enabling, or drive the map through the map path (`PUT /tables/dt_map/data/updates`
with `replace_map: true`, scoped by `map_key_columns`), which does purge.

[What this deliberately does NOT do]

- It does not write `dt_lot`/`dt_slot` into the map. Those arrive later, confirmed.
- It does not fill absent `c_bn`. A cell with no bin is absent, not "good".
- It does not resolve the coordinate frame. `dt_x`/`dt_y` are copied as recorded;
  which of the 8 frames they are in is a separate, declared answer (see
  `docs/spec/TRACE_FIXTURE_SPEC.md` section 4-0-quater). Guessing it here would bake an
  unverified rotation into stored data.
- It says nothing at all when a required column is missing. A partial map is not a
  smaller claim, it is a DIFFERENT claim -- "these dies were not transferred".
"""

from typing import Any, Dict, List

from sqlalchemy.orm import Session

from mappers.base import BaseMapper

# The cell's own address. These are exactly `dt_map`'s composite_key_source, and every
# one must be present or crud cannot compose the key and mints an orphan row instead.
KEY_COLUMNS = ("dt_job", "dt_x", "dt_y")

# The cell's value.
VALUE_COLUMNS = ("c_bn",)

# Provenance carried so a cell can name the core die it came from. This is what makes
# the map traceable rather than merely drawable -- one DT wafer mixes dies from several
# core wafers, so the origin cannot be recovered from the map's own key.
ORIGIN_COLUMNS = ("core_wafer", "core_lot", "core_slot", "core_x", "core_y")


def build_dt_map_batch_df(db: Session, payloads: List[Dict[str, Any]]) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """One `dt_log` row becomes one `dt_map` cell."""
    if not payloads:
        return {"updates": []}

    df = BaseMapper.payloads_to_df(payloads)

    if any(col not in df.columns for col in KEY_COLUMNS):
        return {"updates": []}

    df = df.dropna(subset=list(KEY_COLUMNS))

    carried = [c for c in (KEY_COLUMNS + VALUE_COLUMNS + ORIGIN_COLUMNS)
               if c in df.columns]

    updates = []
    for _, row in df.iterrows():
        cell = {col: row.get(col) for col in carried}
        updates.append({
            # No business_key_val on purpose -- see the module docstring.
            "updates": cell,
            "source_name": "chain_ingestion",
            "updated_by": "chain_worker",
        })

    return {"updates": updates, "silent": False}
